secure-eye (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed
    - Ubuntu packages for resolute, stonking and tenacious failed to build
      on Launchpad: the Debian source package left out every directory named
      `tests`, including the app's own test sources, which the build needs.
    - Fedora COPR builds for RHEL 10 and AlmaLinux 10 failed, because their
      meson is too old for the app. Those two now get `secure-eye` without
      `secure-eye-gui`; Fedora and CentOS Stream still get both.

 -- Vedran Hrabar <vedran.hrabar@outlook.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000

secure-eye (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Added
    - `secure-eye-gui`, the SecureEye app, as its own package on
      Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora and Arch, so headless installs of `secure-eye`
      pull in no Qt. It ships under the app ID
      `io.github.vhrabar.SecureEye`, with a desktop entry, an icon and
      AppStream metainfo. With it you can enroll face models with a live
      camera preview and remove them, run a recognition test that shows how
      close each frame comes to the `certainty` threshold, switch face login
      on and off, edit the configuration, and see and control the
      `secureeye-authd` service and the configured camera.
    - The app works through `secureeye-authd`: the daemon owns the camera
      and the face models, so the app never opens the camera itself and
      needs no administrator rights to manage your own models. A login
      always takes priority; an enrollment or test running in the app is
      stopped when a login needs the camera.
    - `secureEye re-enroll` replaces your face models after a change of
      recognizer, and keeps the models of the previous one so switching back
      still works.
    - `--json` output for `list`, `remove`, `clear`, `disable`, `set` and
      `version`, for scripts.
  * Changed
    - Face models carry the recognizer that made them, and a login never
      compares models from different recognizers. If yours no longer match
      the configured one, the login says so and asks you to run `secureEye
      re-enroll`.
    - Face model files are stored in a new format. Existing files keep
      working and are converted the first time they are changed; older
      releases cannot read converted files, so downgrading after that means
      enrolling again.
    - Face models are owned by the `secureeye` daemon user and are no longer
      readable by other users.
    - The daemon's socket is open to all users, and each request is checked
      against the user who sent it: you can only log in as, or manage the
      models of, yourself (root can do both for anyone). Screen lockers that
      run PAM as the logged-in user can now reach the daemon.
    - The daemon handles several requests at once, so a client that stalls
      no longer holds up everyone else.
  * Fixed
    - Face login failed every time on systems with NumPy 2 (`only
      0-dimensional arrays can be converted to Python scalars`).

 -- Vedran Hrabar <vedran.hrabar@outlook.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000

secure-eye (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed
    - COPR builds failed in the source stage, before any chroot was built:
      `.copr/Makefile` resolved the release version with `git describe`
      while make parsed the file, which happens before the recipe installs
      git. The version came out empty and `set-package-version.sh` aborted.
      It is now resolved inside the recipe, after git is installed, and a
      clone with no reachable tag fails with a message that says so rather
      than with a blank version.

 -- Vedran Hrabar <vedran.hrabar@outlook.com>  Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

secure-eye (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Changed
    - SecureEye now ships as a single `secure-eye` package on Debian, RPM
      and Arch, replacing `libpam-secureeye`, `secureeye-authd` and the
      transitional metapackage. Existing installs are swapped automatically
      on upgrade.
    - The recognition backends are ordinary distribution packages instead of
      a bundled virtualenv: `python3-mediapipe` (amd64/x86_64 only) and
      `python3-dlib`, from `ppa:vhrabar/tools` on Ubuntu and
      `copr:vhrabar/python-extras` on Fedora. Select one with
      `detector_backend` in `/etc/secureEye/config.ini`.
    - The daemon and the `secureEye` launcher run on the system Python
      interpreter, so nothing has to be rebuilt after a Python upgrade. The
      systemd unit and the launcher now use the interpreter meson was
      configured with rather than a hardcoded virtualenv path.
    - Releases no longer carry `.deb` or `.rpm` attachments. Install from
      the PPA, COPR or the AUR instead.
  * Added
    - Arch Linux packaging is published to the AUR as `secure-eye`.
    - `CHANGELOG.md` is the single source of truth for release notes.
      `scripts/set-package-version.sh` renders a release's section into the
      Debian and RPM changelogs, and `scripts/changelog-section.sh` extracts
      it for the GitHub release body.
    - A single release workflow that runs the tests, verifies the deb, RPM
      and Arch packages build and install, and only then publishes to the
      Launchpad PPA, COPR and the AUR. COPR builds are pinned to the
      released tag.
    - A Prepare release workflow that sets the version, tags it and drafts
      the release from the changelog.
  * Removed
    - The install-time virtualenv, the vendored Python wheels and the dpkg
      trigger that rebuilt the virtualenv whenever python3 was upgraded.
  * Fixed
    - Launchpad PPA uploads carry the real changelog entries; every upload
      previously read "New upstream release".
    - Fedora and RHEL install instructions: the package ships no systemd
      preset, so `secureeye-authd.service` has to be enabled manually. The
      README claimed installation enabled it.
    - The CLI reference lists the `set` command.

 -- Vedran Hrabar <vedran.hrabar@outlook.com>  Tue, 18 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

